Let me think about what makes a great pomodoro timer:

1. **Core functionality:**
   - Focus session (25 min default)
   - Short break (5 min default)
   - Long break (15 min default)
   - Start/pause/reset controls
   - Timer countdown with visual progress
   - Session counter (completed pomodoros)
   - Auto-suggest long break after 4 pomodoros (common convention)

2. **Clean UI design:**
   - Minimalist aesthetic
   - Nice typography (tabular numbers for the timer so digits don't jump)
   - A circular progress ring (SVG) showing time remaining
   - Mode tabs (Focus / Short Break / Long Break)
   - Subtle color shifts per mode (focus = red/rose or indigo, breaks = teal/green)
   - Smooth transitions
   - Settings for custom durations
   - Maybe sound notification on completion (Web Audio API beep — no external assets needed)

3. **Extra polish:**
   - Keyboard shortcuts (Space to start/pause, R to reset)
   - Document title updates with remaining time
   - Persist settings in localStorage
   - Progress ring animation
   - Maybe a subtle pulse when running
   - Task label input ("What are you working on?")
   - Session dots showing progress toward long break (4 dots)

Let me design the layout:

- Centered card layout on a soft background
- Header: app title "Pomodoro" with maybe a small logo dot
- Mode switcher: pill-style segmented control with three options
- Big circular timer: SVG ring, time in the middle, session label below
- Controls: primary Start/Pause button, Reset button, Skip button
- Below: session dots (4 dots showing progress to long break)
- Settings: gear icon opens a small panel/modal to adjust durations, auto-start toggles, sound toggle
- Footer: completed sessions count today, keyboard hint

Color scheme: I'll go with a warm, calm palette. Maybe:
- Background: soft off-white / warm gray (#F7F5F2 or similar)
